# AMH — line endings for the files the harness reads byte-for-byte.
#
# Git for Windows sets `core.autocrlf=true` in its SYSTEM config when it installs, so a repo
# with no .gitattributes hands every text file to the worktree with CRLF endings the index
# never had. The harness notices, because several of its rungs compare or parse bytes rather
# than lines: the secret scan compares a file against its own filtered stream, the integrity
# rung reads filenames out of `scripts/MANIFEST.sha256`, and `amh.conf` is sourced by bash,
# where a trailing CR becomes part of the value.
#
# Deliberately narrow. These are the paths AMH installs and reads; what the rest of your tree
# does with line endings stays your decision, and this file is yours to extend. Note that it
# governs future checkouts only: a worktree that is already CRLF stays that way until you
# renormalise it (`git add --renormalize .`, or a fresh clone).
*.sh                    text eol=lf
amh.conf                text eol=lf
scripts/MANIFEST.sha256 text eol=lf
AGENTS.md               text eol=lf
CLAUDE.md               text eol=lf
docs/STATE.md           text eol=lf
docs/RUNBOOK.md         text eol=lf
docs/LEDGER*.md         text eol=lf

# Windows batch files are CRLF by convention; the Gradle wrapper ships that way.
*.bat                   text eol=crlf
